# Proof

**Claim:** Yes, such a function exists. We construct it explicitly and prove it has the required property.

## Construction (Thomae's function)

Define $g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ by

$$
g(x)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{q}, & x=\dfrac{p}{q}\in\mathbb{Q},\;\gcd(p,q)=1,\;q>0,\\[6pt] 0, & x\notin\mathbb{Q}.\end{cases}
$$

We prove:

- **(A)** $g$ is discontinuous at every $x_0\in\mathbb{Q}$, and
- **(B)** $g$ is continuous at every $x_0\notin\mathbb{Q}$.

---

## (A) Discontinuity at rationals

Let $x_0=p/q\in\mathbb{Q}$ in lowest terms, so $g(x_0)=1/q>0$.

Since the irrationals are dense in $\mathbb{R}$, there exists a sequence $(x_n)$ of irrationals with $x_n\to x_0$. For every $n$, $g(x_n)=0$. Hence

$$
\lim_{n\to\infty}g(x_n)=0\neq \frac{1}{q}=g(x_0).
$$

A function continuous at $x_0$ must send every convergent sequence $x_n\to x_0$ to $g(x_n)\to g(x_0)$. This fails, so $g$ is **discontinuous at $x_0$**. $\checkmark$

---

## (B) Continuity at irrationals

Let $x_0\notin\mathbb{Q}$, so $g(x_0)=0$. We show $\lim_{x\to x_0}g(x)=0$, i.e. for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists $\delta>0$ such that

$$
0<|x-x_0|<\delta\;\Longrightarrow\;g(x)<\varepsilon.
$$

**Key observation.** Fix $\varepsilon>0$ and consider the set

$$
S_{\varepsilon}:=\left\{\frac{p}{q}\in\mathbb{Q}\;\middle|\;\gcd(p,q)=1,\;q>0,\;\frac{1}{q}\ge\varepsilon\right\}=\bigcup_{q\le\,1/\varepsilon}\left\{\frac{p}{q}\;\middle|\;\gcd(p,q)=1\right\}.
$$

Only finitely many denominators $q$ satisfy $q\le 1/\varepsilon$, and for each such $q$ there are only finitely many rationals $p/q$ in any bounded interval. Therefore, in any bounded neighborhood of $x_0$, the set $S_\varepsilon$ is **finite**.

**Choosing $\delta$.** Since $x_0$ is irrational, $x_0\notin S_\varepsilon$. The set $S_\varepsilon\cap[x_0-1,x_0+1]$ is finite and does not contain $x_0$, so

$$
\delta:=\min\Big\{\,|x_0-r|\;:\;r\in S_\varepsilon\cap[x_0-1,x_0+1]\,\Big\}\cup\{1\}>0
$$

is well defined and strictly positive (a minimum of finitely many positive numbers).

**Verification.** Take any $x$ with $0<|x-x_0|<\delta\le 1$.

- If $x$ is irrational, $g(x)=0<\varepsilon$. $\checkmark$
- If $x=p/q\in\mathbb{Q}$ in lowest terms, then $|x-x_0|<\delta$ and $x\in[x_0-1,x_0+1]$. By the definition of $\delta$, $x\notin S_\varepsilon$, which means $\frac{1}{q}<\varepsilon$, i.e. $g(x)=\frac{1}{q}<\varepsilon$. $\checkmark$

In both cases $g(x)<\varepsilon$. Therefore $\lim_{x\to x_0}g(x)=0=g(x_0)$, and $g$ is **continuous at $x_0$**. $\checkmark$

---

## Conclusion

The function $g$ (Thomae's function) is continuous at every irrational point and discontinuous at every rational point. Hence **yes, such a function can be constructed.** $\blacksquare$

